<title>TAIWAN: Taiwan starts issuing stock warrants.</title>
<headline>Taiwan starts issuing stock warrants.</headline>
<dateline>TAIPEI 1997-06-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Taiwan's top securities watchdog said it had started accepting applications for the issue of warrants on qualified locally listed firms from Monday.</p>
<p>A Securities and Exchange Commission statement said the warrant issue will be limited to qualified individual stocks, sectors or a basket of shares.</p>
<p>The warrants will be listed in the main Taiwan Stock Exchange, officials have said.</p>
<p>The screening and approval rules became effective from June 1, but since June 1 was Sunday, the rules actually took effect from Monday, a commission official said.  </p>
<p>Local and foreign banks or securities brokerages need to have capital of T$3 billion to issue warrants on local listed firms, finance minister Paul Chiu said in April.</p>
<p>Analysts said a total of 24 listed firms meet the qualification, including Taiwan Cement, Taiwan Tea, President Enterprises, Nan Ya Plastics, Formosa Plastics, China Petrochemical, Hualon, Shinkong Synthetic Fiber, and Tatung.  </p>
<p>Others are Teco, Pacific Electric Wire, Walsin Lihwa, China Steel, United Microelectronics, Acer, First International Computer, Winbond, Cathay Construction, China Development, Taipei Business Bank, Medium Business Bank of Tainan, Taichung Business Bank, Chinatrust Commercial Bank and Farmers Bank of China.</p>
<p>-- Taipei Newsroom (5080815)</p>
